WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.240
Actions on objectives describes the point where the adversary attempts to achieve the

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:05.000
intended impact.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.995
The objective may be data theft, fraud, disruption, sabotage, account abuse,

00:00:08.995 --> 00:00:11.490
ransomware impact, business email compromise,

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:15.205
operational interference, or something specific to the organization.

00:00:15.205 --> 00:00:17.968
Defenders use this stage to connect technical evidence to business risk.

00:00:17.968 --> 00:00:19.000
What data was touched?

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.000
Which privileged actions occurred?

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.000
Which accounts approved changes?

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Did backups remain usable?

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.000
Were transactions altered?

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.250
Did a business process stop working?

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:30.000
Monitoring data access, identity activity, privileged actions,

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.960
payment or workflow changes, and unusual export behavior can help identify impact.

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:37.000
Recovery planning matters here as well.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:39.000
Backups, continuity procedures, transaction review,

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:43.105
and communication plans reduce the chance that a late-stage incident becomes a prolonged

00:00:43.105 --> 00:00:44.000
business crisis.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:46.000
This stage should also feed lessons learned.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.000
If the team only restores service and moves on,

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:49.980
it misses the chance to strengthen controls.

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:54.000
Good analysis of actions on objectives explains what outcome was attempted or achieved,

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.000
what evidence supports that conclusion,

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.000
what business impact remains, and what control improvements should follow.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.000
Late-stage analysis should be tied to business language.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:06.730
A leader may not need every technical detail, but they do need to understand whether data,

00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:11.000
money, availability, safety, trust, or regulatory obligations were affected.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:13.804
That translation helps response priorities match organizational risk.
